      "title": "Hobart and William Smith Colleges",
      "description": "Salisbury Center for Career, Professional and Experiential Education | HWS Skip to main content Hobart and William Smith Colleges Menu Visit Apply Give Search What can we help you find? About HWS About Home Campus Map Mission History Belonging, Equity & Diversity HWS Centers Geneva & the Finger Lakes HWS Leadership Offices/Administration Academics Academics Home Majors & Minors; Pre-Professional & Graduate Programs Curriculum Advising Career Services Faculty Global Education HWS Deans' Offices Research and Honors Experiential Education Admissions Admissions Home Admitted Student Resources Apply Visit Campus Virtual Visit Request Info Students Request Info Parents Financial Aid & Scholarships Admissions Team/Contact Athletics About Athletics Three-peat! Hobart Hockey Wins 2025 National Championship! HWS Athletics Website Life at HWS Life at HWS Home Wellness Dining Belonging, Diversity & Equity Student Engagement Recreation Spiritual Engagement News News Home This Week in Photos Events Calendar President's Forum Campus Community Faculty Landing Page Staff Landing Page Student Landing Page Alumni & Friends Alumni & Friends Home Get Involved Stay Connected Events Reunion Ways to Give Annual Fund News Resources Awards & Honors Travel Program Contact Us Parents & Families Parents & Families Home Stay Connected Ways to Give News Resources for Parents & Families Events Academic Calendar ACTIVITIES AND EVENTS CALENDAR THIS WEEK IN PHOTOS The Melly Institute For Business, Innovation and Leadership Further Together: The Campaign for Our Third Century Cookie preferences This site uses cookies to analyze traffic and improve your experience. See our Privacy Policy for details. Accept Reject Salisbury Center for Career, Professional and Experiential Education Jump to: Career Services Student Resources Guaranteed Internship Program Pathways Program Workshops, Training and Appointments Career Treks and Networking Internship Map Information for Alumni Information for Employers Outcomes Brochure Outcomes 2026 Back to: Home Centers for Experiential Education Career Services The Future We're Focused on is Yours. Contact Salisbury Center for Career, Professional and Experiential Education 300 Pulteney Street Geneva, NY 14456 Phone: (315) 781-3514 Fax: (315) 781-3515 E-Mail: cso@hws.edu Location: The Salisbury Center for Career, Professional and Experiential Education is located on the 1st floor of Trinity Hall Office Hours: Monday-Friday, 8:30 a.m. - 5 p.m. Find upcoming events on Handshake . Through the Salisbury Center for Career, Professional and Experiential Education, you can explore careers, get one-on-one advising and land an internship with guaranteed funding. Yes, that’s right, we guarantee that you will participate in at least one internship or research opportunity—and we have the funding to help pay for it. Our comprehensive, career development program – called Pathways – complements your academic pursuits, helping you connect your interests, strengths and goals to potential professional paths. Rest assured: if you want to explore a career, develop a skill or build your professional network, we’ll be with you every step of the way. 2026 Outcomes Read Our Outcomes Brochure Thinking about your future? So are we. Signature Programs Guaranteed Internship Get on the path to guaranteed funding. We were among the first colleges to guarantee an internship or research placement and it was a shocking idea at the time. Years later, the extent of our offerings and the depth of our connections are second to none. You have the flexibility to pursue incredible opportunities without having to worry about whether you can afford it. Get Experience Pathways Program Our signature program is a comprehensive, four-year sequence of experiences that will help you explore careers, discover your path and prepare for post-college life. Beginning at First-Year Orientation, you'll engage with Career Services and set a plan in motion. Click here to get started. On the Right Track Workshops & Training Career Services offers a variety of workshops and provides access to various training programs. Develop New Skills Career Treks and Networking Go on the road with us! Join us during winter and spring break for programs held in New York, Washington, D.C., Boston and Los Angeles that allow you to network with HWS alums and friends and learn more about particular industries. Job shadowing experiences and informational interviews can be arranged throughout the year. Build Your Network About Us Our dedicated career counselors provide assistance along your career journey. We encourage you to reach out for a one-on-one appointment. Meet the Team Academic Awards and Fellowships HWS students regularly win prestigious fellowships that fund graduate study, international travel, and unique experiences. Previous Winners Pre-Professional Programs Gain both an undergraduate and post-graduate education through HWS' pre-professional programs. Students can take programs in engineering, health professions, business, teaching or law, preparing them for the career of their choosing. Architecture Business Education Engineering Health Professions Law Internships & Outcomes Career-Services Across Disciplines, Across Continents Supported by the Charles H. Salisbury Summer International Internship Stipend, Selina Owino '28 is expanding her professional experience while maximizing companies’ social impact. Career-Services Career Trek Leads to Congressional Internship After visiting Capitol Hill with Career Services, Bella Diamandis ’28 is spending the summer interning in Congressman Jim McGovern's district office. Dartmouth Engineering Joint Degree Program with Dartmouth Maryfei Berrigan ’27 and Jake Romney ’26 reflect on their experience in the HWS-Dartmouth engineering joint-degree program. Read More Internship Map Click the image below to view an interactive map of recent internships. HWS Outcomes Director of the Salisbury Center for Career, Professional and Experiential Education Brandi Ferrara touches base with students who hold internships in Geneva this summer. During a Career Services event, Abby Goodrich ’18, who is an Assistant General Manager for the Bozzuto Group, speaks to students about internship and job opportunities. During Day on the Hill, alums at Education Administration Programs (EAB), including Kristi Augenblick ’12, senior strategic leader; Claire Criniti ’14, associate director, partner development Enroll360; and Corey McClintok Davis ’13, senior director or partner development, marketing & enrollment solutions, answer questions from students. While visiting campus, former Tampa Bay Buccaneer Ali Marpet ’14 gathers for a photo with staff in Saga. During the Day on the Hill program, students gather with Associate Professor of Politics DeWayne Lucas in front of the U.S. Capitol building in Washington, D.C. Students on the Communications Career Trek Program in New York City listen to Screenvision Media Chief Executive Officer John G. Partilla III P’25. With Professor of Economics Tom Drennen, Master of Science in Management students enjoy a Career Trek in Rochester, N.Y. Here, they visit Plug Power Inc. hosted by Operations Controller James Painton ’97, P’25. At Bloomberg in Midtown Manhattan, students and Professor of Economics Feisal Khan hear from Matthew Fox ’19, Joseph Gibbons ’14 and Kelsie Worth ’22 about their careers at the financial software company. Macquarie Group Associates Bowen Wang ’14 and Tucker Randle ’15 speak to students about investment banking and opportunities. Hollie Harnas ‘23, Lauren Earley '23 and Emily Kahn ‘23 work this summer at the National Women's Hall of Fame in Seneca Falls, N.Y. During a visit to campus, award-winning writer, director and producer Brad Falchuk ’93, L.H.D. ’14 discusses his career and answers questions from students in Bartlett Theatre. Students interested in finance careers gather for a photo while visiting 12 banks and firms in NYC, gaining advice from more than 70 alums during winter break. President Mark D. Gearan awards Trustee Bill Whitaker ’73, L.H.D.’97 with the Hobart Alumni Association’s Distinguished Service Award for Lifetime Service during a gathering of alums. The reception was hosted by David and Jane Blum Ebby ’84, P’18 and Bailey Ebby ’18 on Martha’s Vineyard, Ma. President and CEO of Scout Motors Scott Keogh ’91 talks with students, faculty and staff at a dinner at the President’s home prior to his President’s Forum talk. Mark Neveldine '95, the writer, director and producer of films such as Panama, Crank, Gamer and The Vatican Tapes, talks with students interested in working in film. Hobart and William Smith Colleges © 2026 Hobart and William Smith Colleges Geneva, NY 14456 (315) 781-3000 Non-Discrimination Statement ADA Compliance Privacy Policy Campus Map Contact the Colleges Employment HWS on Facebook HWS on Twitter HWS on Instagram HWS on YouTube HWS on LinkedIn HWS on TikTok ©"
    },
    "https://www.hws.edu/academics/faculty.aspx": {
      "title": "Hobart and William Smith Colleges",
      "description": "Our Faculty | Hobart and William Smith Colleges Hobart and William Smith Colleges Menu Visit Apply Give Search What can we help you find? About HWS About Home Campus Map Mission History Belonging, Equity & Diversity HWS Centers Geneva & the Finger Lakes HWS Leadership Offices/Administration Academics Academics Home Majors & Minors; Pre-Professional & Graduate Programs Curriculum Advising Career Services Faculty Global Education HWS Deans' Offices Research and Honors Experiential Education Admissions Admissions Home Admitted Student Resources Apply Visit Campus Virtual Visit Request Info Students Request Info Parents Financial Aid & Scholarships Admissions Team/Contact Athletics About Athletics Three-peat! Hobart Hockey Wins 2025 National Championship! HWS Athletics Website Life at HWS Life at HWS Home Wellness Dining Belonging, Diversity & Equity Student Engagement Recreation Spiritual Engagement News News Home This Week in Photos Events Calendar President's Forum Campus Community Faculty Landing Page Staff Landing Page Student Landing Page Alumni & Friends Alumni & Friends Home Get Involved Stay Connected Events Reunion Ways to Give Annual Fund News Resources Awards & Honors Travel Program Contact Us Parents & Families Parents & Families Home Stay Connected Ways to Give News Resources for Parents & Families Events Academic Calendar ACTIVITIES AND EVENTS CALENDAR THIS WEEK IN PHOTOS The Melly Institute For Business, Innovation and Leadership Further Together: The Campaign for Our Third Century Cookie preferences This site uses cookies to analyze traffic and improve your experience. See our Privacy Policy for details. Accept Reject Faculty Jump to: Academics Majors, Minors, Pre-Professional and Graduate Programs Curriculum Advising Career Services Faculty Global Education Research Academic Calendar Student Learning Objectives Back to: Home Academics You'll be taught and mentored by the best. Literally. HWS has been ranked in the top 25 nationwide for best faculty by The Princeton Review for seven consecutive years. Professors. Scholars. Guides. We have big plans for your ambitions. You’re the reason we’re here, in this place that lets us put our students first. We’re students, too. It’s just that we’ve been at it longer. We don’t have all the answers, but we’ll show you how answers get found. By inviting you to join us in the lab. By challenging your assumptions. By lending our experience as you build your own. We’ll look you in the eye and get to know you as you are. Then we’ll invite you to stand on our shoulders and see what that perspective offers. Search for a Faculty Member Faculty Spotlight Career-Services Putting Economics to Work Professor Joyce Jacobsen launched an SIE exam preparation program for HWS students and delivered the Eastern Economic Association’s presidential address on the economics of higher education. Faculty Expanding the Screen Lisa Patti’s new book brings together more than 40 scholars to explore new approaches to writing about today’s evolving media landscape. Faculty Metz Selected for Senior Leadership Academy Professor of Geoscience and Associate Provost Nicholas Metz will exchange ideas with higher education leaders from across the country through a yearlong leadership program. Faculty-Student Mentorship In This Week in Photos, we showcase our extraordinary faculty, who are recognized as the best by the Princeton Review. Here, Professor of International Relations Kevin Dunn teaches “Introduction to International Relations” in Stern Hall. Assistant Professor of History Janette Gayle holds “Railroad to Freedom: The Underground Railroad in New York” in the Poole Family Admissions Center – the same location where abolitionist Harriet Tubman met with author Sarah Hopkins Bradford, who lived in the home and penned Tubman’s biography in 1869. Associate Professor of Dance and Movement Studies Kelly Johnson leads students in “Intro to Jamaican Dance” outside of the Gearan Center for the Performing Arts. Professor of Chemistry Justin Miller looks on as Jadon Layne '25 pulls pipettes to make thin layer chromatography spotters. Associate Professor of Sociology Kendra Freeman teaches “Data Analytics” in Stern Hall. Associate Professor of Theatre Chris Hatch leads students during rehearsal for “Much Ado About Nothing” in the McDonald Theatre. Professor of Psychological Science Julie Kingery teaches “Topics in Developmental Psychology” on the steps of Coxe Hall. Associate Professor of Politics DeWayne Lucas teaches \"Intro to American Politics\" in Stern Hall. Kevin Frost ’25 and Professor of Biology Meghan Brown examine Bloody Red Shrimp under microscopes in Eaton Hall. Associate Professor of Art and Architecture Stan Mathews gathers with students at the Roman Colosseum during the study abroad program. Professor of Spanish and Hispanic Studies May Farnsworth, Daniella Lorenzana ’26 and other students in “Intermediate Spanish” speak in Spanish while ice skating at the Cooler. With Professor of Economics Tom Drennen, Master of Science in Management students enjoy a Career Trek in Rochester, N.Y. Here, they visit Plug Power Inc. hosted by Operations Controller James Painton ’97, P’25. Led by Associate Professor of Psychological Science Emily Fisher (front center), students explore Arenal Volcano National Park during their J-term course “The Psychology of Consumerism and Sustainability on Latin America” in Costa Rica. During the First Year Seminar “Face to Face,” Visiting Associate Professor of Africana Studies James McCorkle leads a class discussion. Professor of Mathematics and Computer Science Jonathan Forde teaches a statistics class. Professor of Religious Studies Erin Anwar engages with students participating in a group activity during “Ethical Debates in Medicine.” Hobart and William Smith Colleges © 2026 Hobart and William Smith Colleges Geneva, NY 14456 (315) 781-3000 Non-Discrimination Statement ADA Compliance Privacy Policy Campus Map Contact the Colleges Employment HWS on Facebook HWS on Twitter HWS on Instagram HWS on YouTube HWS on LinkedIn HWS on TikTok ©"
    },
    "https://www.hws.edu/offices/deans/default.aspx": {
      "title": "Hobart and William Smith Colleges",
      "description": "Deans | Offices | Hobart and William Smith Skip to main content Hobart and William Smith Colleges Menu Visit Apply Give Search What can we help you find? About HWS About Home Campus Map Mission History Belonging, Equity & Diversity HWS Centers Geneva & the Finger Lakes HWS Leadership Offices/Administration Academics Academics Home Majors & Minors; Pre-Professional & Graduate Programs Curriculum Advising Career Services Faculty Global Education HWS Deans' Offices Research and Honors Experiential Education Admissions Admissions Home Admitted Student Resources Apply Visit Campus Virtual Visit Request Info Students Request Info Parents Financial Aid & Scholarships Admissions Team/Contact Athletics About Athletics Three-peat! Hobart Hockey Wins 2025 National Championship! HWS Athletics Website Life at HWS Life at HWS Home Wellness Dining Belonging, Diversity & Equity Student Engagement Recreation Spiritual Engagement News News Home This Week in Photos Events Calendar President's Forum Campus Community Faculty Landing Page Staff Landing Page Student Landing Page Alumni & Friends Alumni & Friends Home Get Involved Stay Connected Events Reunion Ways to Give Annual Fund News Resources Awards & Honors Travel Program Contact Us Parents & Families Parents & Families Home Stay Connected Ways to Give News Resources for Parents & Families Events Academic Calendar ACTIVITIES AND EVENTS CALENDAR THIS WEEK IN PHOTOS The Melly Institute For Business, Innovation and Leadership Further Together: The Campaign for Our Third Century Cookie preferences This site uses cookies to analyze traffic and improve your experience. See our Privacy Policy for details. Accept Reject Office of the Hobart and William Smith Deans Jump to: Office of the Deans Meet the Deans About Honor Societies HWS Dean's List Academic Resources Course Withdrawals, Leaves & Departing HWS Parent Information First-Year Seminar Dean Model Events Photo Gallery Back to: Home Offices/Administration Office of the Deans Office Hours 8:30 a.m. to 5:00 p.m., Monday through Friday (Summer hours: 8:30 a.m. to 4:00 p.m.) To schedule an appointment with your dean, please contact the office by phone at (315)-781-3467 or by email at hwsdeans@hws.edu . We look forward to connecting with you. Located on the first floor of Smith Hall, the four Hobart and William Smith Deans are available to assist students in many aspects of college academic life. New first-year students are assigned to their dean in their first-year seminar and get to know them as part of that experience; students will continue to be advised by the same dean throughout their time at HWS. Transfer students will be assigned a dean upon admission to HWS. HWS students can meet with their academic dean for guidance on any topic, such as: Successfully transitioning from one academic semester or year to the next Identifying or changing an academic advisor Building scholarly relationships with faculty Becoming more engaged in academic and research opportunities Selecting major and minor programs Achieving academic recognition and attending academic ceremonies Applying to college Honor Societies Participating in campus traditions including Convocation, Blackwell-Hale, and Moving Up Day Planning for study abroad, internship or research experiences Transferring credits Taking a leave of absence Experiencing academic difficulty and understanding the Academic Review process Helping students plan next steps, such as graduate and professional schools Meet the Deans Kelly Payne Senior Associate Dean View Bio DAVID MAPSTONE '93, P'21 Associate Dean View Bio Amy Green Associate Dean View Bio Kelvin D. Clark Assistant Dean View Bio Jessica McDonald Office Manager View Bio About The Office of the Deans HONOR SOCIETIES The Office of the Deans supports honor societies for sophomore, junior and senior students. Honor society members are selected based on their scholarship, leadership, character and service. Typically, they are student leaders who foster a sense of community on campus. Students interested in becoming part of an honor society should inquire about the application process with their Dean. Hobart and William Smith Honor Societies DEAN'S LIST The Committee on Standards has established the following standards for Dean’s List Distinction: Students must complete four full credit courses or their equivalent for the academic semester; at least three of the courses must be taken for grades, with no grades below C-; courses taken for CR/NC must receive a grade of CR; no incomplete initiated by the student for non-medical reason may be taken; and a grade point average of 3.5 must be attained. The Dean's List is calculated each semester. A notation of this honor is made on the student's transcript. HWS Dean's List HWS Scholars Traditions Maintaining traditions serves to connect students to the past, present and future of HWS. The traditions encourage students to understand Hobart and William Smith in a historical perspective. Hobart Traditions William Smith Traditions PARENTS AND FAMILIES The Deans are important contacts for parents and families as they are responsible for the academic and co-curricular well-being of all students. Families should not hesitate t o contact the Office of the Deans with questions or concerns regarding their student’s well-being or academic progress. When appropriate, the Deans can often connect students to necessary resources or facilitate relationships with other parties on campus. News Alums Convocation 2025: \"Put yourself out there. Make yourself heard.\" Convocation called students to collaborate, question and lead with purpose, while recognizing Walt Disney Imagineer Richard “Toby” Pugh ’67 with the Hobart Medal of Excellence. Alums Moving Up Day 2025 Join Moving Up Day 2025 on Friday, May 2 to celebrate the academic achievements and leadership roles of all students. ccesl Unveiling Curiosities at FSEM Symposium First-year students showcase their semester of learning More News Hobart and William Smith Colleges © 2026 Hobart and William Smith Colleges Geneva, NY 14456 (315) 781-3000 Non-Discrimination Statement ADA Compliance Privacy Policy Campus Map Contact the Colleges Employment HWS on Facebook HWS on Twitter HWS on Instagram HWS on YouTube HWS on LinkedIn HWS on TikTok ©"
    },
